00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:45.000
Tina Henares: Yeah.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:16.000
Whitney Hodges: accessibility. Um, so for somebody who may be, uh, like, visually impaired, but is, like, looking at a website and using some sort of, like, tool to help them, like, read or, like, find out what's on the website, the images, like, need to have, like, a name or a description that makes sense to them.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:16.000
Tina Henares: Yeah.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:19.000
Whitney Hodges: You know, if it's just, like, a long string of numbers, like, that'.

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:20.000
Tina Henares: Mm-hmm.

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:40.000
Whitney Hodges: doesn't really mean anything. And I want to say like, I don't know that the laws are like super clear around that yet at this point, but I have heard things before about like companies getting sued for like ADA stuff when their websites like not.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:55.000
Whitney Hodges: compliant in that way, or, like, accessible in that way. So, just want to make sure that for all of our clients' landing pages, we're, like, when you're uploading the photos, we're, like, giving it a name that is, you know, descriptive, um, that kind of, like.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:53.000
